        <div id="impact-vs-drill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Impact Driver vs Drill: What Is the Difference?
          </h2>

          <p>
            An impact driver looks like a short drill, but it works very differently. Instead of a three-jaw chuck it has a 1/4-inch hex quick-release collet that accepts hex-shank bits. Inside, a spring-loaded hammer strikes an anvil many times per second whenever the fastener resists, adding sharp rotational blows to the motor's steady torque. That is why an impact driver can drive a 3-inch screw into dense lumber with little wrist strain, while a drill would stall or twist in your hand.
          </p>

          <p>
            A drill/driver has a clutch that slips at a set torque, which makes it the better tool for repeatable screw depth in soft materials and for drilling accurate holes with round-shank bits. An impact driver has no clutch, so on small screws and soft materials it is easy to overdrive, strip or snap fasteners unless the tool has low-speed modes. The best modern drivers, like most picks here, solve much of that with speed settings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Impact drivers are also louder, and the hammering makes them less precise when drilling. You can drill with hex-shank bits, and for small pilot holes that works fine, but for clean, accurate holes, large spade bits or hole saws, the drill is the right tool. Our <a href="/best-cordless-drill/">best cordless drill</a> guide covers drills in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            So which should you buy? If you can only have one tool, a drill/driver is more versatile. If you build decks, fences, sheds or furniture, drive structural screws or lag bolts, or do a lot of repetitive fastening, an impact driver will save time and wrists, and owning both is the standard setup. A combo kit with a drill and impact driver on one platform is usually the best value in cordless tools.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Drive with the impact, drill with the drill.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Keep a drill bit in the drill and a driver bit in the impact driver, and you will rarely need to swap. The impact handles long screws and lags; the drill handles holes and delicate fastening.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

