        <div id="specs-that-matter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Specs Actually Matter
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why torque ratings are not comparable</h3>

          <p>
            Impact driver torque is measured on a fastener being struck repeatedly, which makes it harder to test consistently than steady motor torque. Brands use their own internal methods and rarely publish the details, including what joint, battery and duration they used. Milwaukee, DeWalt, Makita, Ryobi and Bosch all quote in-lbs, but the numbers are not the result of one shared test. So the Ryobi's 2,200 in-lbs does not mean it out-drives Milwaukee's 2,000 in-lbs, and Makita's 1,600 in-lbs does not mean it is the weakest tool here. Use torque only to compare drivers within one brand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Speed, impact rate and modes</h3>

          <p>
            Top RPM and impacts per minute shape how fast a screw goes in, and they are reported more consistently than torque. Most 18V flagships run around 3,200 to 3,900 RPM. More valuable still are speed settings or modes. A low mode lets you drive small screws without snapping them; a self-tapping or assist mode eases off once a screw starts in metal. Three settings is the minimum we would accept; four is better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Head length and weight</h3>

          <p>
            A short head matters more than you might expect. Between studs, inside a cabinet or under a sink, an extra inch decides whether the tool fits square to the screw. The DeWalt DCF850 is under 4 inches; the Milwaukee 2953-20 is 4.47 inches. Weight matters for overhead work and belt carry. Note that makers quote weight differently, sometimes bare and sometimes with a battery, so compare like with like.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brushless motors and the collet</h3>

          <p>
            All picks here are brushless, which gives better runtime and longevity than older brushed models. Every one uses a 1/4-inch hex quick-release collet. Check that it ejects bits easily and holds them without wobble; owners commonly mention collet feel in reviews, and it is a sign of overall build quality.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="platform" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Match the Impact Driver to Your Drill
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most important rule when buying an impact driver is to buy it on the same battery platform as your drill. Batteries and chargers are the expensive part, and two tools sharing a set of packs is far cheaper than two separate systems. If you own a Milwaukee M18 drill, buy the 2953-20. If you own DeWalt 20V MAX, buy the DCF845 or DCF850. Makita LXT owners should buy the XDT16, Ryobi owners the PBLID02, and Bosch owners the GDR18V-1860CN.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are buying both tools at once, a combo kit is usually the best deal, but check which impact driver is in it. Combo kits sometimes include an older or lower-tier driver to hit a price, so compare the model number against the picks here. Our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a> guide compares what you get across the two biggest pro platforms, including battery sizes and warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryobi ONE+ is the platform for homeowners who want many tools for little money; our <a href="/ryobi-tools-review/">Ryobi tools review</a> covers where it is strong and where it falls short. Milwaukee and DeWalt are the long-term pro choices, and Milwaukee's M12 line adds a second, compact family that is ideal for a 12V drill and impact pair. Makita and Bosch are excellent but smaller in the US.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own a 12V drill and an 18V drill, consider which you reach for most. Many people find the 12V impact driver is the one they use daily, because it is light enough to keep on a belt, and they leave the 18V tools for decks and framing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bits-and-fasteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Bits, Fasteners and Getting Good Results
          </h2>

          <p>
            An impact driver is only as good as the bit in it. Standard screwdriver bits are hard and brittle and will crack under impact blows; use bits labeled impact-rated, which are made to flex slightly and survive. Buy a few of the sizes you use most, usually #2 Phillips, T25 Torx and square drive, because they wear out and a worn bit cams out and strips screw heads.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fastener choice matters too. Torx and square-drive screws grip far better than Phillips under an impact driver, which is why most deck and structural screws use them. For lag screws, use a hex socket adapter, drill a pilot hole sized to the screw's root diameter, and drive in a lower mode until the head seats, then stop. Over-tightening a lag with an impact driver is easy and can strip the wood fibers.
          </p>

          <p>
            For delicate work, such as cabinet hardware, hinges or small screws into softwood, use the lowest speed setting and feather the trigger. If a job needs repeatable screw depth in soft material, switch to a drill with a clutch. For machine fasteners where an exact tightness matters, such as lug nuts or engine parts, an impact driver is the wrong tool entirely; use a properly set <a href="/best-torque-wrench/">torque wrench</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, wear hearing protection for long sessions. Impact drivers are loud, and an afternoon of decking adds up. Eye protection is sensible too, since snapped bits and screw heads can fly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="12v-or-18v" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. 12V or 18V and 20V: Which Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 12V impact driver like the Milwaukee M12 FUEL 3453-20 is smaller and lighter than an 18V model and still has real power. Milwaukee rates it at up to 1,500 in-lbs and lists it at 2 lb, which makes it a pleasure for cabinet installs, electrical work, furniture assembly and general home projects. The trade-off is stamina on heavy work: small batteries and less headroom mean slower progress on long lags and structural screws.
          </p>

          <p>
            An 18V or 20V driver like the 2953-20 or DCF845 is the right choice for decks, fences, framing, sheds and any project with hundreds of long screws. With a 4.0 or 5.0Ah battery, it will run most of a day of decking. The compact 18V models, like the DCF850, narrow the size gap a lot.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only buy one impact driver, make it 18V or 20V on the platform of your drill. If you already have one and work a lot in tight spaces, a 12V driver is a great second tool, and paired with a 12V drill, it makes a light everyday kit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Impact Driver Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you already own batteries, buy that brand's driver from this list: the 2953-20 or 3453-20 for Milwaukee M18 or M12, the DCF845 or DCF850 for DeWalt 20V MAX, the XDT16 for Makita LXT, the PBLID02 for Ryobi ONE+, and the GDR18V-1860CN for Bosch 18V.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are starting from scratch, choose a platform first with our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a> guide, then buy a drill and impact driver together. For long-term pro use, the Milwaukee 2953-20 with the 2903-20 drill, or a DeWalt DCF845 with the DCD800, are both strong pairs. For homeowners on a budget, the Ryobi PBLID02 with the PBLDD01 drill gives you two brushless tools and the widest cheap tool family. For tight spaces, the M12 FUEL pair is hard to beat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, use impact-rated bits, keep the low mode for small screws, and let the drill handle the holes. Our <a href="/best-cordless-drill/">best cordless drill</a> guide covers the other half of the pair.
          </p>
