        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing torque ratings across brands</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each maker measures impact torque by its own method and rarely publishes the details. A 2,200 in-lbs rating from one brand does not beat a 2,000 in-lbs rating from another. Compare torque only within one brand, and judge across brands on speed, modes, size and platform.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using standard bits in an impact driver</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ordinary screwdriver bits are brittle and crack under impact blows, then cam out and strip screw heads. Buy impact-rated bits in the sizes you use most and replace them when the tips round over. They are cheap, and a worn bit wastes screws and time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving small screws on full power</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An impact driver has no clutch. On top speed it will snap small screws, strip soft wood and crack trim. Use the lowest mode for small fasteners and cabinet hardware, feather the trigger, or switch to a drill with a clutch when screw depth matters.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an impact driver for precise torque</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Impact drivers are not torque-controlled tools. Never use one to tighten lug nuts, engine fasteners or anything with a specified torque. Run fasteners down with the driver if you like, then finish with a properly set torque wrench.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a combo kit without checking the driver model</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some combo kits include an older or lower-tier impact driver to hit a price. Check the model number in the listing against current models before you buy. A slightly pricier kit with the current flagship driver is often the better long-term value.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
