          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best impact driver for the money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, the Ryobi PBLID02 offers the best value. It has a brushless motor, four modes including an Assist mode for self-tapping screws, and a rated 2,200 in-lbs of torque, on the ONE+ platform with hundreds of affordable tools. If you are on a pro platform already, the best value is always the driver that fits your existing batteries.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an impact driver better than a drill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is better at driving long screws, structural fasteners and lag bolts, and it is easier on your wrist. A drill is better at drilling accurate holes, running large bits, and driving small screws to a repeatable depth thanks to its clutch. If you can only buy one, buy a drill; if you build anything, own both.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use an impact driver as a drill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For small pilot holes with hex-shank drill bits, yes, and it works well. For clean, accurate holes, large spade bits, hole saws or masonry, no. The hammering action makes it less precise, and it cannot hold round-shank bits. Use a drill/driver for real drilling and keep the impact driver for fastening.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much torque do I need in an impact driver?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Every 18V or 20V driver on this list has enough for deck screws, structural screws and moderate lag bolts. Because each brand rates torque differently, do not shop by headline torque across brands. Look at speed modes, size and platform instead. A 12V driver is enough for cabinets, electrical work and most household projects.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Milwaukee or DeWalt better for impact drivers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both make excellent impact drivers. Milwaukee's 2953-20 has four drive modes and a short head; DeWalt offers the full-power DCF845 and the very short Atomic DCF850. The bigger decision is the battery platform, which our Milwaukee vs DeWalt guide covers in detail. Buy the driver that matches your drill's batteries.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need special bits for an impact driver?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Use bits labeled impact-rated, which are designed to survive repeated rotational blows. Standard bits are harder and more brittle and can crack or shatter. You also need hex-shank bits, because the impact driver's 1/4-inch hex collet cannot hold round-shank bits. Torx and square-drive bits grip better than Phillips.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a brushless impact driver worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Brushless motors run cooler, get more work from each battery charge and generally last longer than brushed motors, and they are standard on every current mid-range and pro impact driver. All seven picks here are brushless. A brushed driver is only worth considering at the very cheapest end, for occasional light use.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
